          "level": 2,
          "content": "2.8 Special Types of Angles"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.456,
            0.439,
            0.544
          ],
          "content": "Let us go back to Vidya’s notebook and observe her opening the cover of the book in different scenarios."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.546,
            0.439,
            0.635
          ],
          "content": "She makes a full turn of the cover when she has to write while holding the book in her hand."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.637,
            0.442,
            0.703
          ],
          "content": "She makes a half turn of the cover when she has to open it on her table. In this case,"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.459,
            0.456,
            0.813,
            0.682
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_14_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.705,
            0.832,
            0.75
          ],
          "content": "observe the arms of the angle formed. They lie in a straight line. Such an angle is called a **straight angle**."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.273,
            0.756,
            0.673,
            0.803
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.429,
                0.802,
                0.501,
                0.818
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 2.11"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_14_image_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.82,
            0.831,
            0.866
          ],
          "content": "Let us consider a straight angle $\\angle \\mathrm { A O B }$ . Observe that any ray $\\overrightarrow { \\mathrm { O C } }$ divides it into two angles, $\\angle AOC$ and $\\angle \\mathrm { C O B }$"
        },
